# Build context hygiene for every image built from this repository.
#
# Two jobs, in order of importance:
#   1. Keep secrets OUT of image layers. A `COPY . .` in any Dockerfile would
#      otherwise bake a developer's real `.env` into a layer that is pushed,
#      shipped over SSH, and readable by anyone who can `docker history` the
#      image (Constitution Principle X: secrets are resolved at runtime, never
#      passed at build time).
#   2. Keep the context small, so `docker build` stays fast and layer caching
#      is not busted by files the build never reads.

# --- Secrets (rule 1). Order matters: the negation must follow the pattern. ---
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# --- Build output and dependency trees (regenerated inside the image) ---
node_modules
**/node_modules
dist
**/dist
build
**/build
# The generated Prisma client. Excluded even though it lives under src/, not
# dist/ (packages/persistence/prisma/schema.prisma's `generator` comment
# explains why it's generated there instead of node_modules/.prisma) — every
# stage that needs it runs its own `prisma generate` from postinstall, and
# without this, spec 003's `COPY packages/persistence/src/` in the deps stage
# would bake in whatever a contributor's own machine happened to have
# generated locally, defeating that reproducibility entirely.
**/src/generated
.turbo
**/.turbo
*.tsbuildinfo
coverage
**/coverage

# --- Version control and CI ---
.git
.gitignore
.github

# --- Documentation and process artefacts: never read by a build ---
adr
specs
docs
diagrams
.specify
.claude
*.md
!README.md

# --- Local tooling and editor state ---
.vscode
.idea
.DS_Store
*.log
npm-debug.log*
pnpm-debug.log*

# --- Docker's own files: changing a compose file must not bust the build cache ---
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore
